EXT. FLOATING MARKET OF BELLS - DUSK

A market built on rafts lashed together across a slow green river, each stall hung with a thousand tiny bells that ring in the evening wind. Lanterns drift on the water. The air tastes of cinnamon and wet rope.

At the center of the flotilla, a single raft has gone quiet. Its awning sags. Its bells hang unmoving, as though holding their breath.

ILSEBETH CROWE, 40s, in a coat stitched from sailcloth, stands at its edge with a long pole. Across the water-gap, a smaller raft drifts nearer, poled by TALLIS ARVEN, 20s, barefoot, his hair full of salt.

                    ILSEBETH
(to herself)
Steady. Steady.

She braces. The raft thumps against hers. Tallis catches a rope and ties it off with the careless speed of one who has done it ten thousand times.

                    TALLIS
Evening, Captain.

                    ILSEBETH
Not Captain anymore.

                    TALLIS
That's not what the river says.

                    ILSEBETH
The river doesn't say anything.

                    TALLIS
It says plenty. You just don't listen.

She turns to the crowd gathered on the neighboring decks. Fishwives, tinkers, a juggler stilled mid-toss, a child on a father's shoulders. All watching.

                    ILSEBETH
(to all)
Good people of the Bells. A word.

The crowd murmurs. A bell somewhere rings once, and falls silent.

                    ILSEBETH (CONT'D)
The river is dropping. Six inches since dawn. The old channel closes in three days.

Gasps. Someone drops a basket.

                    ILSEBETH (CONT'D)
We can wait for rain. Or we can leave tonight.

A woman near the front calls out. Ilsebeth lifts a hand.

                    ILSEBETH (CONT'D)
No. I won't ask you to follow me. I'll ask you to choose.

She turns, back to Tallis, and lowers her voice.

                    ILSEBETH (CONT'D)
(quiet)
Did the ferrymen come?

                    TALLIS
Yes.

                    ILSEBETH
How many?

                    TALLIS
Nine. They'll tow us as far as the weir.

                    ILSEBETH
And past it?

                    TALLIS
Past it, we're on our own.

She nods. Looks at the water for a long time. Then she looks up at the awning where the bells hang motionless.

                    ILSEBETH
(muttering)
Strange. They always ring when I lie.

                    TALLIS
Maybe you're not lying.

                    ILSEBETH
Maybe I'm not.

She lifts the pole. Sets it against the planks. Pushes.

Across the market, one by one, the ropes are cast off. The bells begin to ring, a slow wave that runs along the whole flotilla, lantern to lantern, raft to raft, until the entire river sings.

                    TALLIS
(grinning)
There it is.

Ilsebeth does not smile. But she stands straighter, and her hands, on the pole, are steady.
